n Hand: A hand which would otherwise be worth of zero fans, with flower tiles not included. Example: Hand: [W1, W2, W3, T7, T8, J2, J2] + T9, Melds: [[W3, W4, W5], [B2, B2, B2]]\\n        * Last Tile Draw: The winning hand is completed by drawing the last tile of the wall.\\n        * Last Tile Claim: The winning hand is completed by seizing a tile from another player when the tile wall is empty.\\n        * Out With Replacement Tile: The winning hand is completed when drawing a tile right after making a concealed kong or a promoted kong.\\n        * Robbing the Kong: The winning hand is completed by robbing the tile which another player adds to an exposed pung to form a kong.\\n    Patterns worth 6 points:\\n        * All Pungs: A hand with four pungs or kongs, regardless of whether they are exposed or concealed.\\n        * Half Flush: A hand consisting of only honor tiles and suited tiles of one suit. Example: [W1, W2, W3, W5, W5, W5, W6, W7, W8, F1, F1, F1, J3, J3]\\n        * Mixed Shifted Chows: A hand with three consecutive chows in different suits, each shifted up one number from the one before it. Example: [W3, W4, W5, B4, B5, B6, T5, T6, T7, W5, W5, W5, B9, B9]\\n        * All Types: A hand containing tiles of all three suits, wind tiles and dragon tiles. Example: [W1, W2, W3, B4, B4, B4, T7, T8, T9, F2, F2, F2, J1, J1]\\n        * Melded Hand: Every meld and pair in the hand must be completed by tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be exposed, and one tile of the pair is the 14th winning tile from other players.\\n        * Two Concealed Kongs: A hand with two concealed kongs.\\n        * Two Dragon Pungs: A hand with two pungs or kongs of dragon tiles.\\n    Patterns worth 4 points:\\n        * Outside Hand: A hand with terminals (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) and honor tiles in every meld and the pair.\\n        * Fully Concealed Hand: A hand with no tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be concealed, and the winning tile is drawn by the winner.\\n        * Two Melded Kongs: A hand with two exposed kongs.\\n        * Last Tile: The winning tile is the last tile of the same tile that has not been revealed to all players, which means three of the same tile has been exposed in melds or discarded on the table.\\n    Patterns worth 2 points:\\n        * Dragon Pung: A hand with a pung or kong of dragon tiles. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Prevalent Wind: A hand with a pung or kong of the prevalent wind of this game.\\n        * Seat Wind: A hand with a pung or kong of the seat wind of the current player.\\n        * Concealed Hand: A hand with no tiles from other players except the winning tile, which means that all melds must be concealed, while the winning tile is seized from other players.\\n        * All Chows: A hand with four chows and a pair of suited tiles.\\n        * Tile Hog: A hand with four identical tiles without forming a kong. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple tiles meeting this requirement.\\n        * Mixed Double Pung: A hand with two pungs or kongs in different suits with the same number. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Two Concealed Pungs: A hand with two concealed pungs or kongs.\\n        * Concealed Kong: A hand with a concealed kong.\\n        * All Simples: A hand with no terminal tiles (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) and honor tiles.\\n    Patterns worth 1 points:\\n        * Pure Double Chow: A hand with two identical chows. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Mixed Double Chow: A hand with two chows in different suits with the same number. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Short Straight: A hand with two chows in the same suit with successive numbers, like 234 and 567. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Two Terminal Chows: A hand with two chows in the same suit with numbers 1, 2, 3 and 7, 8, 9. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Pung of Terminals or Honors: A hand with a pung or kong of terminal tiles (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) or honor tiles. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Melded Kong: A hand with one exposed kong.\\n        * One Voided Suit: A hand with exactly two of the three suits of tiles.\\n        * No Honors: A hand with no honor tiles.\\n        * Edge Wait: The winning tile must be a suited tile of number 3 to complete a chow of 1, 2, 3, or number 7 to complete a chow of 7, 8, 9. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Closed Wait: The winning tile must be a suited tile to complete a chow as the middle number. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Single Wait: The winning tile must be the tile to complete a pair. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Self Drawn: The winning tile must be drawn by the winner.\\n    Now is your turn, to your best ability, please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format, such as Play B5.\\n        Your response format should be exactly:\\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\\n    \", \"user_prompt\": \"The following is the state information: The Prevailing Wind is: South Wind.Your Seat Wind is: South Wind.\\nUnshown tiles are: [W1: 4; W2: 4; W3: 4; W4: 4; W5: 4; W6: 4; W7: 4; W8: 4; W9: 4; T1: 4; T2: 3; T3: 3; T4: 2; T5: 3; T6: 1; T7: 2; T8: 1; T9: 4; B1: 4; B2: 4; B3: 4; B4: 4; B5: 4; B6: 4; B7: 4; B8: 4; B9: 4; F1: 4; F2: 4; F3: 4; F4: 4; J1: 4; J2: 4; J3: 4].\\nYour hand tiles are: [W2: 1; W5: 1; W9: 3; T3: 1; B4: 1; B7: 1; F1: 1; J1: 2].\\nYour melds are: [[T4, T5, T6]].\\nThe melds of the next player: [[T6, T7, T8]].\\nThe melds of the player sitting across: [[T6, T7, T8]].\\nThe melds of the previous player: [[T2, T3, T4]].\\nYour game history is: Play T8, Chi T5.\\nThe game history of the next player is: Chi T7, Play T8.\\nThe game history of the player sitting across is: Chi T7, Play T2.\\nThe game history of the previous player is: Play T8, Chi T3, Play T5.\\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play W9; Play B7; Play B4; Play F1; Play T3; Play W2; Play J1; Play W5]\\u3002\", \"reasoning\": \"\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: Several of my tiles can contribute to potential high-scoring patterns, but my current hand is fragmented. I have a concealed pair of J1 (Dragon), a triplet of W9, and a single chow meld in T4-T5-T6. The W9\\u00d73 could form a pung for scoring patterns like \\\"Pung of Terminals or Honors\\\" (1 point) or contribute to \\\"All Terminals and Honors\\\" (32 points) if more terminal/honor melds are formed. However, I lack coordination between suits. The T4-T5-T6 meld is already exposed, and others have revealed T6/T7/T8 chows, meaning T6 and T7 are heavily used\\u2014T6 only has 1 left unseen, T8 only 1, so drawing those is unlikely.\\n\\nMy hand has multiple singles: W2, W5, B4, B7, F1, T3\\u2014all weak and unconnected. J1 pair is valuable as a potential pung (dragon pung = 2 points minimum). W9\\u00d73 is the strongest element. Since I already have a meld, building around W9 pung and aiming for All Terminals and Honors (if I can form more terminal/honor melds) or simply maximizing fan via dragon and wind pairs is viable.\\n\\nDiscarding a safe and low-value tile now is key. Among options:\\n- Play W9: Dangerous \\u2014 I have three, discarding one exposes it early; others may be waiting on W9.\\n- Play B7/B4/W2/W5/T3: All middle/low suited tiles, not part of any strong pattern yet, and no chows formed.\\n- F1: Wind tile, could support Prevalent/Seat Wind pattern (South is prevailing and seat wind, not East/F1), so F1 is irrelevant for wind bonus. But honor pungs are worth something.\\n- J1: Have two, discarding one risks breaking future pung, but currently safe since no one has shown interest.\\n\\nBest discard should be a lone suited tile with least potential. B7 and B4 are isolated; no sign of bamboo chows forming. T3 is also isolated. W5 is in Character suit where I have W2, W5, W9 \\u2014 very spread out, no immediate chow possibility.\\n\\nReason: Among all unpaired, non-terminal, non-honor tiles, B7 and B4 are weakest. B7 is a 7-dan, which might be useful in upper-half hands, but no evidence others are building that. Similarly, B4 is mid-number. However, B7 has fewer copies left? No \\u2014 unshown shows all B7 still available (4), so not rare. But safety and irrelevance matter most.\\n\\nW2 and W5 are in the same suit as W9 triple \\u2014 if I ever draw W1/W3/W8/W7, I could form chows, but currently no connection. Still, keeping W2/W5 offers *some* flexibility in Characters. T3 is in Bamboo? No \\u2014 T is Bamboo? Wait: T is Bamboo (\\u6761), B is Dots (\\u997c). Correction:\\n\\n- T3: Bamboo-3\\n- B4: Dot-4\\n- B7: Dot-7\\n\\nI have only one tile in each of these suits outside my existing meld.\\n\\nThus, T3, B4, B7 are all useless singles in suits where I have no other presence. Among them, T3 is a terminal (number 1 or 9? No \\u2014 T3 is Bamboo-3, not terminal). Terminals are 1 and 9. So T3 is simple, not terminal.\\n\\nBut wait \\u2014 previous player has meld [[T2, T3, T4]], so they completed a chow using T3. That means T3 was discarded by someone and they called it. So T3 is known to be somewhat safe \\u2014 they just used it. However, playing T3 now might still be okay \\u2014 they already have their set.\\n\\nStill, better to keep tiles that could form pairs. F1 is a single wind \\u2014 no value unless I get more. J1 I already have two \\u2014 very valuable. W9\\u00d73 is gold \\u2014 do NOT discard.\\n\\nSo weakest tiles are B7, B4, W2, W5, F1, T3.\\n\\nBut note: I previously played T8, and did Chi T5 \\u2014 meaning I formed the T4-T5-T6 chow from a discard. So my hand evolved.\\n\\nNow, looking at possible waits: none obvious.\\n\\nBest move is to discard a tile that is least likely to help me and safest.\\n\\nB7 stands out \\u2014 dot-7, no sequence, no pair, no kong. Same for B4. But B7 is closer to terminal (9), so slightly more dangerous? Not really.\\n\\nHowever, consider: Unshown tiles show T4: 2 left, T5: 3, T6: 1 \\u2014 T6 is almost gone (only 1 left), so no chance to extend chow.\\n\\nNo benefit in holding T3, B4, B7.\\n\\nBut wait \\u2014 is there a knitted straight possibility? Knitted Straight uses 1,4,7 or 2,5,8 or 3,6,9 across suits. I have:\\n- T3 (3)\\n- B4 (4)\\n- W5 (5)\\n- T6 (in meld, 6)\\n- B7 (7)\\n\\nThat\\u2019s interesting \\u2014 T3, B4, W5, T6, B7 \\u2014 that's a partial knitted sequence: 3-4-5-6-7 across suits. Could this lead to Lesser or Greater Honors and Knitted Tiles?\\n\\nLesser Honors and Knitted Tiles requires 14 tiles from the set: 1,4,7 of one suit; 2,5,8 of another; 3,6,9 of third; plus honors.\\n\\nHere, I have:\\n- T3 (3 of Bamboo)\\n- B4 (4 of Dots)\\n- W5 (5 of Characters) \\u2014 but 5 is not in {1,4,7}, {2,5,8}, {3,6,9} directly \\u2014 wait, yes: 5 belongs to {2,5,8}\\n\\nSo:\\n- If T3 is part of {3,6,9} in Bamboo\\n- B4 is part of {1,4,7} in Dots\\n- W5 is part of {2,5,8} in Characters\\n\\nThen I have one from each group. Also have B7 (7 of Dots) \\u2014 fits {1,4,7} in Dots.\\n\\nAnd T6 (6 of Bamboo) \\u2014 fits {3,6,9} in Bamboo.\\n\\nSo actually, I have:\\n- From {1,4,7}: B4, B7\\n- From {3,6,9}: T3, T6\\n- From {2,5,8}: W5\\n\\nThis is promising for **Knitted Straight** (12 points) or **Lesser Honors and Knitted Tiles** (12 points).\\n\\nIf I can collect more tiles from these groups, especially:\\n- Need more {1,4,7}: e.g., B1, B4, B7 (already have), W1/W4/W7, T1/T4/T7\\n- {2,5,8}: W2/W5/W8, T2/T5/T8, B2/B5/B8\\n- {3,6,9}: T3/T6/T9, B3/B6/B9, W3/W6/W9\\n\\nI already have W2, W5 \\u2014 W2 would fit {2,5,8} in Characters.\\n\\nSo W2 is actually useful!\\n\\nSimilarly, W5 is good.\\n\\nT3 is good.\\n\\nB4 and B7 are both in {1,4,7} of Dots \\u2014 very relevant.\\n\\nF1 is honor \\u2014 could help Lesser/Greater version.\\n\\nJ1\\u00d72 \\u2014 dragon, also helps.\\n\\nEven W9 \\u2014 9 is in {3,6,9} \\u2014 T3, T6, T9 are in that group \\u2014 W9 is Character-9 \\u2014 so also in {3,6,9}!\\n\\nWait \\u2014 W9 is Character-9 \\u2014 belongs to {3,6,9} of Characters.\\n\\nSo I have:\\n- {1,4,7}: B4, B7\\n- {2,5,8}: W2, W5\\n- {3,6,9}: T3, T6 (meld), W9\\u00d73\\n\\nThis is **extremely promising** for **Knitted Straight** or **Lesser Honors and Knitted Tiles**.\\n\\nIn fact, with W9\\u00d73, I could make a pung of {3,6,9} group.\\n\\nTo complete Knitted Straight: need one full set of {1,4,7}, {2,5,8}, {3,6,9} \\u2014 but not necessarily all in hand \\u2014 melds count.\\n\\nCurrently:\\n- {3,6,9}: I have T6 (from meld), W9\\u00d73 (pung!), and T3 \\u2014 so plenty\\n- {1,4,7}: B4, B7 \\u2014 missing one more, say B1 or another B4/B7\\n- {2,5,8}: W2, W5 \\u2014 missing W8 or another\\n\\nSo if I keep W2, W5, T3, B4, B7, I am building toward Knitted Straight (12 points) or even Lesser Honors and Knitted Tiles (if I add honors).\\n\\nBut I must discard one.\\n\\nAmong the valid actions, which tile breaks this least?\\n\\nAll of B7, B4, T3, W2, W5, etc. are part of this structure.\\n\\nBut F1 is a single wind \\u2014 not part of {1,4,7}, {2,5,8}, {3,6,9} \\u2014 and not helpful for knitted patterns unless going for Lesser/Greater, but even then, honors are separate.\\n\\nSimilarly, J1\\u00d72 is dragon \\u2014 helpful for Lesser/Greater.\\n\\nBut F1 alone \\u2014 no pair, not prevalent or seat wind (both are South/F2), so F1 (East) gives no wind bonus.\\n\\nTherefore, **F1 is the least useful tile** in my hand for any high-scoring pattern.\\n\\nWhereas every other tile contributes to the knitted structure.\\n\\nEven W9\\u00d73 \\u2014 I must keep.\\n\\nJ1\\u00d72 \\u2014 keep for dragon pair/pung.\\n\\nSo best discard is **F1**.\\n\\nAnswer: Play F1\"}"}
