 be formed by claiming tiles from others \u2014 our current melds qualify.\n\nRemaining hand: Need two more melds and a pair. Current single tiles: W1, W2, W9x3, T1, T5, B1.\n\nBest opportunity:\n- Use **W9x3** as a pung \u2192 already a concealed pung! That gives us a strong base.\n- So our hand structure is: [T4,T5,T6], [J1,J1,J1], [W9,W9,W9], plus singles.\n\nBut wait \u2014 you only have **three W9**, yes \u2014 so you can treat them as a concealed pung.\n\nThus, your hand is effectively:  \nMelds: [T4,T5,T6], [J1,J1,J1], [W9,W9,W9] (concealed), and remaining tiles: W1, W2, T1, T5, B1\n\nYou need one more meld and a pair.\n\nPotential strategies:\n- Try to form a chow with W1-W2-W3 \u2192 need W3\n- Or make another pung via draw/claim\n\nHowever, among valid actions, you must **discard** one tile.\n\nNow consider safety and scoring:\n\n- **Discarding W9** would break a concealed pung, reducing potential for patterns like All Pungs (6 pts), Triple Pung (if other pungs match), or even Little Three Dragons (but J1 is present, J2/J3 gone).\n- But we already Peng'd J1 \u2014 so dragon pung is exposed.\n- Seat Wind and Prevailing Wind are both **South (F2)**. We do **not** have F2. In fact, F2: 2 unshown, but we don\u2019t have it. So no seat wind pung.\n- But if we keep W9 pung, we have one honor/dragon pung (J1), and one terminal pung (W9) \u2014 contributes to Outside Hand? Possibly.\n\nLet\u2019s evaluate viable patterns:\n\n- **All Pungs (6 pts)**: Possible if we turn remaining tiles into pungs. But most are singles. Unlikely.\n- **Outside Hand (4 pts)**: Requires every meld and pair to contain terminals or honors. Our [T4,T5,T6] is **middle tiles**, not terminals or honors \u2192 **invalidates Outside Hand**.\n- **Half Flush (6 pts)**: One suit + honors. We have W and T and B \u2014 multiple suits. But if we go Character-heavy? Currently only W9 pung and W1/W2 \u2014 too weak.\n- **Melded Hand (6 pts)**: Yes! All our melds were formed by Chi/Peng. If we complete the hand by claiming the winning tile (via discard), and all melds are exposed, this qualifies. Already have two exposed melds, and W9 pung is concealed \u2014 **but if we never declare Gang or Peng on W9, it remains concealed**, which would disqualify Melded Hand.\n\nAh! Critical point:\n\n> **Melded Hand**: \"every meld and pair in the hand must be completed by tiles from other players\" \u2192 meaning **all melds must be exposed**.\n\nSo if we keep the W9s as a concealed pung, we **cannot** claim Melded Hand.\n\nBut if we **discard a W9**, then we no longer have a pung \u2014 unless we later Peng or Gang it when someone discards W9.\n\nBut currently, we have three W9s in hand \u2014 it's **already** a concealed pung.\n\nTo qualify for **Melded Hand**, we must **expose** all melds via Chi/Peng/Gang from other players.\n\nOur [T4,T5,T6] was formed via Chi T5 \u2192 exposed \u2705  \n[J1,J1,J1] via Peng \u2192 exposed \u2705  \n[W9,W9,W9] is concealed \u274c \u2192 **would block Melded Hand**\n\nTherefore, to pursue **Melded Hand (6 pts)**, we must **break the concealed pung** \u2014 i.e., discard a W9 so we don't have three.\n\nAlternatively, accept that Melded Hand is off the table.\n\nBut let's see: can we get other points?\n\nOther possibilities:\n- **Dragon Pung (2 pts)**: We have J1 pung \u2192 qualifies.\n- **No Simples?** No \u2014 we have T4,T5,T6 \u2014 not all simples.\n- **One Voided Suit?** We have W, T, B \u2192 three suits \u2192 no.\n- **All Chows?** No \u2014 we have a pung \u2192 no.\n\nWait \u2014 we have a pung (J1), so cannot be All Chows.\n\nBack to strategy.\n\nBetter idea: aim for **Chicken Hand (8 pts)** \u2014 a default 8-point hand that scores nothing else, but still wins.\n\nBut Chicken Hand is only valid if the hand scores **0 fans otherwise** \u2014 so if we can get any pattern worth more than 0 fans, we shouldn't call it Chicken Hand.\n\nBut here, we already have:\n- Dragon Pung (J1): 2 pts\n- Could get Concealed Hand? No \u2014 we used Chi and Peng \u2192 exposed melds \u2192 not fully concealed.\n- Self Drawn? Only if we draw the last tile ourselves.\n- Melded Hand: 6 pts \u2014 possible if all melds are exposed and we win by stealing.\n\nBut again, current W9x3 is concealed \u2192 blocks Melded Hand.\n\nSo to enable Melded Hand, we must avoid having any concealed pung \u2014 thus, **discard a W9**.\n\nBut do we have other ways to score?\n\nAlternative: **All Terminals and Honors**? No \u2014 T4,T5,T6 are middle tiles.\n\n**Full Flush**? No \u2014 mixed suits.\n\n**Half Flush**? We have W, T, B \u2014 too many suits.\n\nSo best path: maximize chance to win with at least 8 points.\n\nCurrently, if we keep W9 pung, we have:\n- Dragon Pung (J1): 2 pts\n- Concealed Pung (W9): could count as Two Concealed Pungs? No \u2014 only one concealed pung. T4T5T6 and J1J1J1 are exposed.\n- So only one concealed pung possible (W9) \u2192 Two Concealed Pungs requires two \u2192 no.\n\nSo only 2 points so far \u2014 not enough.\n\nNeed more.\n\nWhat about **Mixed Shifted Pungs**? No.\n\nOr **Triple Pung**? Need three pungs of same number \u2014 we have J1 (number irrelevant), W9 (9), need another pung of 9 or 1? No.\n\nUnlikely.\n\nPerhaps **Chicken Hand** is acceptable if no better option.\n\nBut we can try to build toward **Pure Double Chow** or similar.\n\nWait \u2014 we have W1 and W2. If we draw or claim W3, we can form W1-W2-W3 chow.\n\nThen we\u2019d have:\n- [T4,T5,T6] (chow)\n- [J1,J1,J1] (pung)\n- [W1,W2,W3] (chow)\n- And need two more sets.\n\nStill far.\n\nAlternatively, focus on discarding safely while keeping options open.\n\nBest safe discard:\n- **T5**: middle tile, not terminal, not honor. T6/T7/T8 are all heavily used \u2014 chows of T5-T6-T7 are unlikely now because T6/T7 are exhausted (T7: 0 left, T6: 0 left). So T5 is very safe \u2014 cannot complete any chow anymore.\n- Similarly, **B1**: B7 is gone (0 left), so B5-B6-B7 is dead. B1-B2-B3 possible, but B2: 2 left, B3: 4 \u2014 possible, but B1 is not highly dangerous.\n- **W1/W2**: could complete W1-W2-W3. W3: 4 unshown \u2014 plenty available. So discard W1 or W2 risks allowing chi if next player has W3 and one of the others. But next player just played W6, W8 \u2014 seems to be dumping Winds. Be cautious.\n\nLook at what others have melded:\n- Next player: [T6,T7,T8], [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 focused on T and W mid-range.\n- Across: [T6,T7,T8], [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 heavy on T7/T8 \u2014 all gone.\n- Previous: [T2,T3,T4], [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 finished B5-B6-B7.\n\nSo B7: 0 left, T7: 0, T8: 0, T6: 0 \u2014 all gone.\n\nSo **T5** is completely safe \u2014 no one can use it for chow (T4,T5,T6: T6 gone; T5,T6,T7: T6/T7 gone).\n\nSimilarly, **W8**: gone (0 left), so W6-W7-W8 or W7-W8-W9 cannot be formed.\n\nBut we don't have W8.\n\nBack to **W1 and W2**: W3 is available (4 left), so W1-W2-W3 is possible. Next player might be collecting W \u2014 they have W4,W5,W6. Maybe going for W3-W4-W5? But W3 is available. So if we discard W1 or W2, and next player has W3 and the other, they can Chi.\n\nFor example, if we discard W2, and they have W1 and W3, they can form [W1,W2,W3] via Chi.\n\nBut they already have [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so possibly building upper/lower sequence? Possible.\n\nSo **W1 and W2 are risky to discard**.\n\nSimilarly, **T1**: T2 has only 1 left, T3: 2 left \u2014 T1-T2-T3 possible, but T2 is rare. Still, previous player had T2,T3,T4 \u2014 maybe out of T1? Unclear.\n\nBut T1 is terminal \u2014 higher chance to be useful.\n\n**B1**: B2: 2 left, B3: 4 \u2014 B1-B2-B3 possible. But previous player just discarded B6, B5 \u2014 seems done with B. Still, across or others?\n\nBut B5,B6,B7 all gone \u2014 so no overlap.\n\nOverall, **T5 is the safest discard** \u2014 middle tile, no possible chow completion due to missing T6/T7.\n\nAlso, T5 appears only once in hand, not part of any potential set.\n\nMoreover, discarding T5 does not break any potential pung or strong chow.\n\nWhereas W9x3 is a strong concealed pung \u2014 worth keeping for potential **All Pungs (6 pts)** + **Dragon Pung (2 pts)** = 8 pts minimum if we can get another pung.\n\nCan we get another pung?\n\nPossible: draw or claim W1, W2, T1, B1 \u2014 but all singles.\n\nBut if we keep W9 pung, and later Peng or Gang another set, we might reach All Pungs.\n\nFor example, if someone discards T1, and we have another T1, we could Peng \u2014 but we only have one.\n\nSame for others.\n\nAlternatively, if we draw a duplicate.\n\nBut chances low.\n\nStill, **keeping W9 pung** gives us:\n- Concealed pung (potential for Two Concealed Pungs if another forms, but unlikely)\n- Terminal pung (W9): counts as **Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 pt)**\n- Dragon pung: 2 pts\n- So 3 pts so far \u2014 need more.\n\nBut if we complete hand with Self Drawn (1 pt), Melded Kong (no), etc.\n\nHard to reach 8.\n\nAlternatively, **discard W9** to break concealment, hope to Peng it later \u2014 then it becomes exposed, enabling **Melded Hand (6 pts)**.\n\nAnd we already have two exposed melds \u2014 if we form third via Peng W9, and fourth via Chi or Peng, then win by stealing \u2014 Melded Hand (6 pts) + Dragon Pung (2 pts) = 8 pts.\n\nPerfect.\n\nSo strategy: **discard a W9 now**, so we don't have a concealed pung. Then, if someone discards W9 later, we can Peng it \u2192 expose the pung \u2192 all melds exposed \u2192 qualify for Melded Hand upon winning by steal.\n\nIf we keep W9 in hand, we cannot qualify for Melded Hand even if we win.\n\nTherefore, **discarding W9 enables a path to 8+ points via Melded Hand + Dragon Pung**.\n\nIs there a safer way?\n\nBut W9 is a terminal \u2014 someone might need it for their hand.\n\nBut prevailing wind is South (F2), not W9 \u2014 so not prevalent wind.\n\nSeat wind same.\n\nJ1 pung is dragon \u2014 already scored.\n\nRisk of discard: if someone has two W9, they could win by claiming it.\n\nBut unshown W9: 4 \u2014 means 4 still in wall or hand \u2014 total 4 copies, you have 3, so only 1 left \u2014 so no one can have two W9 unless they drew it.\n\nCheck: total W9: 4 copies.\n\nYou have 3 \u2192 only 1 left in game.\n\nSo no one can have two W9 \u2014 maximum one.\n\nSo **no one can Peng or win with W9 right now**.\n\nTherefore, **discarding W9 is safe** \u2014 cannot be Peng\u2019d, cannot complete a kong.\n\nOnly possible use is for chow? No \u2014 W9 is terminal, can only be in W7-W8-W9, but W7: 2 left, W8: 1 left \u2014 possible? Yes.\n\nCould someone complete W7-W8-W9 by claiming W9?\n\nYes \u2014 if they have W7 and W8.\n\nBut W8: only 1 left \u2014 possibly in hand.\n\nBut is it likely? Possible, but rare.\n\nStill, **risk exists**.\n\nBut compared to T5 \u2014 completely safe.\n\nBut T5 doesn\u2019t help strategy.\n\nWe must balance safety and hand development.\n\nGiven that:\n- Discarding W9 enables **Melded Hand** path (critical for scoring)\n- Only 1 W9 left \u2192 no Peng possible\n- Only risk is **Chi** for W7-W8-W9\n\nBut who has W7/W8?\n\nPrevious player discarded W7 and W8 \u2014 so likely not collecting.\n\nNext player has W4,W5,W6 \u2014 maybe going for W3-W4-W5 or W6-W7-W8? They discarded W8 \u2014 so probably not.\n\nAcross discarded W7, W5 \u2014 seems dumping.\n\nSo W7/W8 likely not being collected.\n\nTherefore, **W9 discard is relatively safe**.\n\nMoreover, keeping three W9s in hand limits flexibility.\n\nBest move: **Play W9**\n\nThis begins the process of forming an exposed pung later, enabling Melded Hand.\n\nReason: Enables the Melded Hand pattern (6 points) by preventing a concealed pung, and with the existing exposed melds, creates a viable path to reach 8 points when combined with Dragon Pung (2 points). The discard is relatively safe given only one W9 remains unaccounted for, minimizing risk of immediate loss.\n\nAnswer: Play W9\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: Several discards are possible, but Play W9 strategically abandons a concealed pung to enable the Melded Hand pattern, which requires all melds to be exposed. Other discards like T5 are safer but hinder scoring potential.\nReason: Discarding W9 prevents the hand from having a concealed pung, allowing future exposure of a W9 pung via Peng if another W9 is discarded, which is essential for qualifying for Melded Hand (6 points). Combined with the existing Dragon Pung (2 points), this provides a clear path to meet the 8-point minimum. The risk is low as only one W9 remains unseen, making a pung impossible for others, and chow usage (W7-W8-W9) is unlikely given opponents' discards.\nAnswer: Play W9"}
