 applies here because without major patterns, it defaults to 8 points if nothing else scores.\n  - However, note: **Seat Wind is South (F2)**, and I do *not* have a pung of F2. Prevailing Wind is also South. But I don\u2019t have any wind pungs.\n  - I have a pung of J1 (Dragon), which gives **Dragon Pung (2 pts)**.\n  - My hand includes multiple suits and honors \u2192 possibly qualifies for **All Types (6 pts)**?\n    - All Types: must have all three suits, dragons, and winds.\n    - I have: W (Characters), T (Bamboos), B (Dots)? Yes \u2014 [T4,T5,T6] is Bamboo, [W1,W2,W3] is Character, [B5,B6,B7] by opponent suggests Dots exist \u2014 but wait, do *I* have a Dot tile?\n    - My hand currently holds B6, and I just discarded B6 recently? Wait \u2014 current hand says [T1:1; B6:1] \u2192 yes, I hold B6.\n    - Melds: none of my own melds use Dots. But the definition of **All Types** requires the *hand* to contain at least one tile from each group: one suit tile from each of the three suits, one wind, one dragon.\n    - I have:\n      - Characters: yes (W1,W2,W3)\n      - Bamboos: yes (T4,T5,T6)\n      - Dots: yes (B6 in hand)\n      - Dragons: yes (J1 pung)\n      - Winds: ? Do I have any wind tile? I previously discarded F1, F2, F3, F4. But did I ever have a wind pung? No. But having even a single wind tile isn't enough \u2014 need a meld or pair? No \u2014 pattern says \"a hand containing tiles of all three suits, wind tiles and dragon tiles\" \u2014 does \u201ccontaining\u201d mean possessing at least one instance?\n    - According to MCR rules, **All Types** requires at least one set (meld or pair) involving each type: i.e., a meld in each suit, a wind meld, and a dragon meld. Or more precisely, per official definition: *\"One pung/kong of dragon, one pung/kong of prevailing wind, and one chow or pung in each suit.\"*\n    - Actually, checking standard MCR: **All Types (Yaojiu Ke)** requires:\n      - One meld with terminals or honors (already satisfied),\n      - Specifically: one pung of dragon, one pung of wind, and one chow/pung in each suit.\n    - I lack a wind pung \u2192 cannot get All Types.\n  - Therefore, likely only **Dragon Pung (2 pts)**, maybe **Outside Hand?** Let's check:\n    - **Outside Hand**: every meld contains at least one terminal (1/9) or honor.\n    - My melds:\n      - [T4,T5,T6]: all simples \u2192 violates Outside Hand \u2192 \u274c\n    - So not Outside Hand.\n  - Best bet: **Chicken Hand (8 pts)** as default minimum.\n- Now evaluate discards:\n  - **Play B6**:\n    - B6 is relatively dangerous \u2014 previous player used it in a Chow ([B5,B6,B7]), and they might be waiting on B6 to complete something? Unlikely since they already completed it.\n    - Also, B6 appears only once unshown \u2192 very few left.\n    - But holding onto B6 blocks me from winning unless I draw another B6 (very unlikely).\n    - Better to discard it now and hope T1 becomes the pair when I draw a second T1.\n  - **Play T1**:\n    - T1 has 3 copies still unshown \u2192 higher chance someone will discard it, or I may draw it.\n    - However, if I discard T1, then I'm trying to pair B6 \u2014 but B6 is nearly extinct.\n    - So keeping T1 is safer.\n\nBut wait \u2014 I already have [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 if I keep T1, can I form another chow? T1,T2,T3? But I don\u2019t have T2/T3. T2 is all gone (unshown: T2:0). So no chance of forming T1-T2-T3. Thus T1 cannot form a chow. So its only value is as a pair.\n\nSimilarly, B6: could it form a chow? B4,B5,B6? B4:2 unshown, B5:1 \u2192 possible, but I don\u2019t have them. B5,B6,B7? Previous player had that. So no.\n\nThus, both T1 and B6 can only serve as pair material.\n\nSo which tile has higher survival rate?\n\n- T1: 3 unshown \u2192 high chance one remains in wall.\n- B6: only 1 unshown \u2192 extremely low chance.\n\nTherefore, **keep T1**, discard B6.\n\nIf I draw a T1 later, I can win with pair of T1.\n\nAlternatively, if someone discards T1, I can claim it to win.\n\nWhereas B6 is almost impossible to get again.\n\nHence, best move: **Play B6**\n\nReason:  \nB6 has only one copy remaining unshown, making it highly unlikely to draw a second B6 for a pair. In contrast, T1 has three copies still available, offering a much better chance to complete a pair either by drawing or claiming. Discarding B6 maximizes the probability of eventually winning with a T1 pair, while minimizing reliance on a nearly exhausted tile.\n\nAnswer: Play B6"}
