her discard completes a legal hand with a pair** \u2014 unless one of the melds is actually a concealed kong, making it 13 tiles in melds (one kong = 4 tiles, others = 3\u00d73=9 \u2192 total 13), plus 1 hand tile \u2192 then draw makes 2.\n\nWait \u2014 possibility: one of your melds is a **kong** but displayed as triplet?\n\nLook at your melds: [J1, J1, J1] \u2014 could this be a kong? You had Peng J1 earlier, and later no Gang action, but possible.\n\nBut no \u201cGang\u201d in your history \u2014 only \u201cPeng J1\u201d.\n\nSimilarly, Peng W9 \u2014 [W9,W9,W9] \u2014 same.\n\nBut unshown J1: 0 \u2014 all 4 J1 are gone. You have 3 in meld \u2014 who has the fourth?\n\nYour meld: [J1,J1,J1] \u2192 3 tiles\nUnshown: J1: 0 \u2192 so 4th J1 must be elsewhere.\n\nBut no other player has J1 in their melds \u2014 so missing.\n\nLikewise, W9: you have 3, unshown: 0 \u2192 total 4, so 4th W9 must be somewhere.\n\nPossibility: you promoted a pung to kong? But no \u201cGang\u201d in history.\n\nYour history: \u201cPeng J1\u201d, \u201cPeng W9\u201d \u2014 both pungs formed via peng.\n\nLater no gang \u2014 so likely still pungs.\n\nBut tile count doesn\u2019t add up \u2014 unless the system assumes exposed kong is still shown as triplet in meld list.\n\nIn many systems, exposed kong is recorded as [tile,tile,tile] in melds, with the fourth added separately.\n\nBut here, melds are shown as triplets.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, assume your melds are all triplets \u2014 so 12 tiles.\n\nHand: 2 tiles \u2192 14 total \u2192 you must discard one.\n\nBut you have **no pair**, so you cannot win.\n\nTherefore, you must be in a transitional state \u2014 perhaps you just drew a tile and now must discard.\n\nYour goal: discard safely, considering what others might be waiting for.\n\nNow evaluate safety of T1 vs T4:\n\n- **T1**: \n  - Unshown: 3 remain (out of 4)\n  - Previous player has [T2,T3,T4] and [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 likely building middle sequences.\n  - Next player has [T6,T7,T8] \u20