infer you won\u2019t discard it unless forced.\n\nBut you\u2019re discarding one of two singles.\n\nFinal consideration: **scoring potential**\n\nCurrent hand structure:\n- Melds: Two Chows, Two Pungs (one dragon, one wind)\n- Hand: T1, T4\n\nAfter discarding:\n- If you keep T4 and discard T1: still no pair\n- If you keep T1 and discard T4: same\n\nSo you're not close to winning \u2014 must wait for pair.\n\nBut you might be aiming for **All Types** (8 points): requires all three suits, winds, dragons.\n\nYou have:\n- Dots (W): yes\n- Bamboos (T): yes\n- Characters (B): yes (via [B5,B6,B7] in previous player? No \u2014 wait, you have no B tiles in melds.\n\nYour melds:\n- T: [T4,T5,T6]\n- J1 (dragon)\n- W9 (wind)\n- W: [W1,W2,W3]\n\nSo suited: T (Bamboos), W (Characters) \u2014 missing B (Dots)\n\nHonors: Dragon (J1), Wind (W9) \u2014 but W9 is not prevailing/seat wind \u2014 seat and prevalent is F2 (South)\n\nYou have no F2.\n\nSo **missing Dots suit** \u2014 so not All Types.\n\nAlternative: **Half Flush** (6 points) \u2014 one suit + honors.\n\nYou have two suits (W and T) \u2014 so not Half Flush.\n\n**All Pungs** (6 points): you have two pungs, two chows \u2014 not applicable.\n\n**Dragon Pung** (2 points): you have J1 pung \u2014 counts.\n\n**Prevalent Wind** and **Seat Wind**: both South (F2) \u2014 but you don\u2019t have F2 pung.\n\nYou have W9 pung \u2014 W is Character, 9 is terminal \u2014 so **Pung of Terminals or Honors** (1 point).\n\nBut overall score likely below 8.\n\nYou need to improve.\n\nBut with only two single tiles and no pair, your best bet is to discard safely and hope to draw a useful tile.\n\nBetween T1 and T4:\n\n- **T1 is safer** because T2 is extinct \u2014 no one can complete a chow involving T1.\n- **T4** is the last unseen \u2014 more dangerous.\n\nAlso, you already have a T4 in a meld \u2014 discarding another T4 shows you have extras \u2014 but opponents may suspect you're giving up on T4 set.\n\nBut safety first.\n\nReason: T1 is extremely safe due to T2 being fully discarded (no player can form [T1,T2,T3] without T2), whereas T4 is the last unseen tile and could potentially complete a chow or pung for an opponent, especially since multiple players are active in the Bamboo suit.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
